# Never push to a shared branch

Agents must **never** push directly to `develop` or `main` on `origin`.
`develop` is the integration branch; `main` carries released source.

## Required workflow

1. Create or switch to a **feature branch** (never commit intended work only on
   a shared branch if it will be pushed).
2. Commit on that branch with `git commit -s` — every commit needs a DCO
   sign-off trailer (see `commit-sign-off.mdc`).
3. Push the **feature branch** only: `git push -u origin HEAD` or
   `git push -u origin <branch-name>`.
4. Open a **pull request** into `develop`. Do not merge unless the user
   explicitly asks.

## Explicit bans

- `git push … develop`, `git push … main`, `git push … HEAD:develop`, or any
  refspec that updates `refs/heads/develop` or `refs/heads/main` on a remote.
- Force-pushing a shared branch unless the user **explicitly** requests that
  exact force update in the current message (and even then, prefer
  `--force-with-lease` and confirm the target SHA).
- Treating “commit and push” as permission to push a shared branch. That phrase
  means push the **current feature branch**.

## If already on a shared branch locally

When the user asks to commit/push and `git branch --show-current` is `develop`
or `main`:

1. Create a feature branch from the commits (or before committing).
2. Push that branch.
3. Point the user at a pull request URL.

Do **not** push a shared branch “just this once.”

